<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Han Lin'er</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1366</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Han Lin'er was the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus">White Lotus</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-shantong">Han Shantong</a>, who launched the Red Turban revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> in 1351. In 1355 rebel commanders proclaimed him emperor of a restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> rose under his banner. He drowned in 1366 while being brought to Zhu, who founded the Ming two years later.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Han Lin'er's father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-shantong">Han Shantong</a>, came from a family of hereditary sect leaders and declared himself the King of Light, the saviour awaited by a millenarian belief that combined elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. He claimed to be a seventh-generation descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Emperor Huizong</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. The authorities arrested him, and his supporters rose at Yingzhou on 28 May 1351. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-futong">Liu Futong</a> the rebels called themselves the Red Turban Army and vowed to restore the Song. Han Lin'er went into hiding after his father's arrest, and the Yuan government finally executed Han Shantong in January 1355.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In March 1355 Han Lin'er arrived in Bozhou and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-futong">Liu Futong</a>'s support, was crowned emperor of the Great Song, with the era name Longfeng (Dragon and Phoenix) and the religious title Lesser King of Light. Real power lay with his ministers: Liu soon had the first prime minister, Du Zundao, assassinated and took his place. Rebels at Hezhou submitted to the Song, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, confirmed as their third in command, led them to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in 1356. Song armies raided as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, the Yuan summer capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Shangdu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and the regime set up five provinces between 1356 and 1359.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> power peaked in 1357–1358. In June 1358 Liu took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, the old Song capital, and Mao Gui attacked the Yuan capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>. But the commanders acted independently and fought one another, and in September 1359 the Yuan general Chaghan Temur drove Han from Kaifeng. With only a few hundred soldiers left, the court survived at Bozhou and then Anfeng.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In February 1363 troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-shicheng">Zhang Shicheng</a>, together with Mongol forces, attacked Anfeng and killed Liu. Zhu Yuanzhang rescued Han, against the advice of the Confucian official Liu Ji, and moved his court to Chuzhou, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. Han lived there under Zhu's control and drowned in the Yangtze on a journey to Nanjing. Zhu afterwards removed mention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and the Red Turbans from official documents, suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus">White Lotus</a> faith and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> the state ideology.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Han Lin'er” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Amid the Red Turban Rebellion against the Yuan, <b>Han Lin'er</b>, a claimed descendant of Emperor Huizong of Song, was proclaimed emperor at Bozhou.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-16">16 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1355 his general Liu Futong enthroned Han Shantong's son <b>Han Lin'er</b> as emperor of a restored Song at Bozhou in Anhui.</blockquote><a href="/state/han-song">Han Song</a></li></ul></section>
